# Example: A Simple RNA-seq Workflow

This example demonstrates a typical RNA-seq workflow on WSP. It is intended to illustrate how the concepts introduced throughout this handbook fit together into a complete analysis.

The workflow consists of:

```text
Download FASTQs
        │
Create project
        │
Transfer data
        │
Run FastQC
        │
Run MultiQC
        │
Trim reads
        │
Align
        │
Count reads
        │
Results
```

---

# Step 1: Create a project

Move to your scratch directory and create a new project.

```bash
cd /scratch/<username>

mkdir rnaseq_example
cd rnaseq_example

mkdir raw_data processed_data scripts results logs
```

Your project should now look like:

```text
rnaseq_example/
├── raw_data/
├── processed_data/
├── results/
├── scripts/
└── logs/
```

---

# Step 2: Obtain sequencing data

If your data is already on your computer, transfer it to the `raw_data` directory.

For example:

```bash
scp -r reads/ username@141.48.90.77:/scratch/<username>/rnaseq_example/raw_data/
```

Alternatively, you can download public datasets directly onto WSP.

```bash
cd raw_data

wget https://example.org/sample_R1.fastq.gz
wget https://example.org/sample_R2.fastq.gz
```

Verify that the files are present:

```bash
ls -lh
```

Example output:

```text
sample_R1.fastq.gz
sample_R2.fastq.gz
```

---

# Step 3: Create a Conda environment

Create an environment containing the required software.

```bash
conda create -n rnaseq fastqc multiqc fastp hisat2 samtools subread
```

Activate the environment:

```bash
conda activate rnaseq
```

---

# Step 4: Assess read quality with FastQC

Create an output directory.

```bash
mkdir results/fastqc
```

Run FastQC on all FASTQ files.

```bash
fastqc raw_data/*.fastq.gz \
    -o results/fastqc
```

FastQC generates HTML reports for each sample.

---

# Step 5: Summarize reports with MultiQC

```bash
multiqc results/fastqc \
    -o results/multiqc
```

Open the generated HTML report to obtain a summary of sequencing quality across all samples.

---

# Step 6: Trim adapters and low-quality bases

Create an output directory.

```bash
mkdir processed_data/trimmed
```

Run fastp.

```bash
fastp \
    -i raw_data/sample_R1.fastq.gz \
    -I raw_data/sample_R2.fastq.gz \
    -o processed_data/trimmed/sample_R1_trimmed.fastq.gz \
    -O processed_data/trimmed/sample_R2_trimmed.fastq.gz \
    -h results/fastp.html \
    -j results/fastp.json
```

The trimmed FASTQ files will be used for downstream analysis.

---

# Step 7: Align reads to the reference genome

First build a HISAT2 index (only required once per reference genome).

```bash
hisat2-build genome.fa genome_index
```

Align the reads.

```bash
hisat2 \
    -x genome_index \
    -1 processed_data/trimmed/sample_R1_trimmed.fastq.gz \
    -2 processed_data/trimmed/sample_R2_trimmed.fastq.gz \
    -S processed_data/sample.sam
```

Convert the SAM file to a sorted BAM file.

```bash
samtools view -bS processed_data/sample.sam \
| samtools sort \
-o processed_data/sample.sorted.bam
```

Index the BAM file.

```bash
samtools index processed_data/sample.sorted.bam
```

---

# Step 8: Count reads

Count the number of reads assigned to each gene.

```bash
featureCounts \
    -a annotation.gtf \
    -o results/gene_counts.txt \
    processed_data/sample.sorted.bam
```

The resulting count matrix can be used for downstream differential expression analysis in R or another statistical environment.

---

# Final project structure

```text
rnaseq_example/
├── raw_data/
│   ├── sample_R1.fastq.gz
│   └── sample_R2.fastq.gz
│
├── processed_data/
│   ├── trimmed/
│   ├── sample.sorted.bam
│   └── sample.sorted.bam.bai
│
├── results/
│   ├── fastqc/
│   ├── multiqc/
│   ├── fastp.html
│   ├── fastp.json
│   └── gene_counts.txt
│
├── scripts/
└── logs/
```

---

# Summary

This workflow demonstrates the basic steps of a typical RNA-seq analysis:

1. Organize your project.
2. Transfer or download sequencing data.
3. Create a software environment.
4. Assess read quality with FastQC.
5. Summarize quality reports with MultiQC.
6. Trim adapters and low-quality bases.
7. Align reads to a reference genome.
8. Count reads for downstream analysis.

Although real analyses often include additional quality control and statistical analyses, these steps form the foundation of many RNA-seq pipelines.
